/* ==============================================================
   04 — Posture configuration: eight ways to reach one pose
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     Meca500 only (R3 or R4)
   TEACHES   SetConf, SetAutoConf
   NEEDS     Robot activated + homed, plenty of clear space
   WATCH     The tool tip stays put while the arm rearranges
   TIME      ~40 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THE THING NOBODY MENTIONS UNTIL IT BITES

   A pose says where the tool tip is and how it is oriented. Six
   numbers. But a six-jointed arm can usually reach that same
   pose in EIGHT different ways — elbow up or elbow down,
   shoulder left or right, wrist flipped or not.

   Same tool tip. Same orientation. Completely different-looking
   robot.

   Three parameters describe which one:

       SetConf(cs, ce, cw)

     cs   shoulder   -1 or 1
     ce   elbow      -1 or 1
     cw   wrist      -1 or 1

   Two options each, three parameters, eight combinations.

   WHY YOU SHOULD CARE

   The eight postures are geometrically equivalent and
   practically very different:

     - one may collide with a fixture and another may not
     - one may have room to reorient and another may be against
       a joint limit
     - one may be far from a singularity and another right on top
       of one
     - and if the robot silently changes posture between two
       cycles, the arm swings through a large, unexpected motion
       to get there — the classic "it moved somewhere strange"
       report

   BY DEFAULT: AUTOMATIC

   Automatic posture selection is on by default. The robot picks
   the joint set closest to where it currently is, which is
   almost always the sensible choice and keeps motion small.

   Leave it on for demos and for free-space motion. Turn it off
   when you need a guarantee.

       SetAutoConf(1)    robot chooses (default)
       SetAutoConf(0)    freeze the current posture
       SetConf(cs,ce,cw) demand a specific posture
                         (this also switches automatic off)

   THE RULE OF THUMB

   In a real cell, pin the configuration with SetConf at the
   start of the program. It costs one line and it removes an
   entire category of intermittent, hard-to-reproduce faults —
   the ones where a cell runs for three weeks and then one cycle
   takes the long way round.

   MoveLin has a stricter rule: a linear move holds its
   configuration for the whole path and will not flip elbow or
   wrist partway. If a MoveLin is refused for no obvious reason,
   the start and end postures disagreeing is a prime suspect.

   THIS EXAMPLE MOVES A LOT

   Switching posture means large arm motions. Give the robot
   room and run it slowly the first time.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode — so this
   program starts from firmware defaults no matter what ran
   before it. Reactivating brings the motors back.

   This does NOT move the arm: a robot that was already homed
   needs no re-homing. (With an MEGP 25* gripper fitted, the
   gripper re-homes on reactivation. The robot still does not
   move — but keep the fingers clear.)

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision and calibration config all
   survive. Run common/cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
Home()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


SetJointVel(15)

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(1)


/* --- Automatic: the default ------------------------------------
   The robot reaches this pose whichever way is closest to where
   it already is. Run the file twice from different starting
   positions and it may well choose differently — which is the
   behaviour we are about to remove. */
SetAutoConf(1)
MovePose(160, 0, 220, 0, 90, 0)
Delay(2)


/* --- The same pose, four demanded postures ---------------------
   Every MovePose line below is IDENTICAL. Only the SetConf above
   it changes. The tool tip goes to the same point in space every
   time, and the arm gets there folded a different way.

   This is the demo. Cover the code, let them watch the arm
   rearrange itself four times, and then reveal that the motion
   command never changed. */

SetConf(1, 1, 1)
MovePose(160, 0, 220, 0, 90, 0)
Delay(2)

SetConf(1, -1, 1)
MovePose(160, 0, 220, 0, 90, 0)
Delay(2)

SetConf(1, 1, -1)
MovePose(160, 0, 220, 0, 90, 0)
Delay(2)

SetConf(1, -1, -1)
MovePose(160, 0, 220, 0, 90, 0)
Delay(2)


/* --- Pinning it for a real program -----------------------------
   This is the pattern to actually use. Choose the posture once,
   at the top, and every pose below is reached the same way,
   every cycle, forever. */

SetConf(1, 1, 1)

MovePose(180, -50, 250, 0, 90, 0)
MovePose(180, 50, 250, 0, 90, 0)
MovePose(180, 0, 280, 0, 90, 0)


// --- Hand control back and go home ----------------------------
SetAutoConf(1)
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. The four-posture sequence is one of the best visual demos
      the robot has. It needs no fixture and no tool, and it
      explains inverse kinematics without the word being said.

   2. Ask an integrator whether their current robot ever "takes a
      strange route" between two taught points. If yes, this is
      usually the reason, and SetConf is the fix.

   3. Point out that the robot checks reachability BEFORE it
      starts moving. Demanding a posture the pose cannot be
      reached in produces an error and a stationary robot, not a
      half-finished move.

   NEXT   05_turn_configuration.mxprog — the same idea for the
          last joint, and the one that saves cables.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
